[{"role": "assistant", "content": "The simplest way to implement continuations in Scheme interpreter written in C is using the setjmp() and longjmp() functions provided by C for non local jumps, they are part of setjmp.h. 

At the lowest level, a continuation is a generalization of a stack: whereas a stack keeps track of where a computation "will go next" when it's finished, a continuation also includes a snapshot of what the stack looked like when the computation was frozen.

Each time a continuation point is reached, where the execution might jump back, you can use setjmp() to save the current execution state including stack, and registers into a structure or 'jmp_buf'. Later on, at some arbitrary point in the execution of your program, you can use longjmp() to jump back to the corresponding setjmp() continuation point. Then all registers, including the PC (program counter), will be restored automatically.

Here is a skeleton of how this might look:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <setjmp.h>

jmp_buf saved_context;

void function_that_longjmps() {
    longjmp(saved_context, 1);
}

int main() {
    if (setjmp(saved_context)) {
        printf("Continuation invoked!\n");
    } else {
        printf("setjmp() for the first time\n");
        function_that_longjmps();
    }
    return 0;
}
```

However, use these functions sparingly, they can mess with control flows and make your code hard to understand and debug. Also, it's not fully portable for all environments and systems due to the differences in ABI's and stack layouts.

If you want to make your interpreter more robust and portable you could look into writing your own stack based system, or using more advanced techniques with your existing stack system. Also, looking into libraries oriented towards C coroutine or green threads implementations can help as these problems are already mostly solved in those ecosystems. Beware though, these topics can get pretty complicated really fast."}]

