[{"role": "assistant", "content": "First, we need to get a clear picture of what a linked list node structure might look like:

```c
struct Node {
  int data;
  struct Node* next;
};

typedef struct Node Node;
```

To find the length of a linked list without using counter or recursion, you can use the "Slow & Fast pointer" technique (also known as the "Floyd's Cycle Detection Algorithm"). It basically uses two pointers which move at different speed through the list. The slow pointer moves one node at a time while the fast pointer moves two nodes at a time. 

To determine both the cycle and the length, you can use the following function. If the list has a cycle, it will return -1 as length. Else, it will return the length of the list.

```c
int findLength(struct Node* head) {
   struct Node *slow_ptr = head, *fast_ptr = head;
   int isCycle = 0; // flag to check if there is cycle in the list
   int length = 0;

   while(fast_ptr != NULL && fast_ptr->next != NULL) {
       slow_ptr = slow_ptr->next;
       fast_ptr = fast_ptr->next->next;
       length++;

       if(slow_ptr == fast_ptr){
          isCycle = 1;
          break;
       }
   }
   /* Now if there is a cycle in the list (slow_ptr == fast_ptr), then we simply return -1 */
   if(isCycle)
      return -1;

   /* Else, append the remaining length by iterating through list with slow_ptr */
   while(slow_ptr != NULL) {
       slow_ptr = slow_ptr->next;
       length++;
   }

   return length;
}
```

We can also create a function specifically to detect cycle in a linked list:

```c
int hasCycle(struct Node* head) {
   struct Node *slow_ptr = head, *fast_ptr = head;

   while(fast_ptr != NULL && fast_ptr->next != NULL) {
       slow_ptr = slow_ptr->next;
       fast_ptr = fast_ptr->next->next;

       if(slow_ptr == fast_ptr)
          return 1; // list has a cycle
   }
   return 0; // list doesn't have a cycle
}
```

Note that if the list has a cycle, we are bound to get stuck in an infinite loop if we just traverse the list to find its length by naively following the "next" pointers from each node. Hence, finding out whether there's a cycle or not in the list becomes a prerequisite for finding its length. The Floyd's cycle-finding algorithm works in O(n) time complexity, where n is number of elements in the linked-list."}]

