However, for investigation and debugging purpose, one can access the memory of other processes. In macOS, you can use the `mach_vm_read()` and `mach_vm_write()` functions provided by the Mach kernel. Here is a simplified example:

```c
#include <mach/mach.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int main(int argc, char** argv) {
    int pid;
    sscanf(argv[1], "%d", &pid);

    mach_port_t process_to_write;
    kern_return_t error;

    error = task_for_pid(mach_task_self(), pid, &process_to_write);

    if ((error != KERN_SUCCESS) || !MACH_PORT_VALID(process_to_write)) {
        printf("Error %d. Please check if pid exists and if taskgated is configured correctly.\n", error);
        return -1;
    }

    vm_offset_t readMem;
    uint32_t size;

    error = vm_read(process_to_write, 0x1000, 0x100, &readMem, &size);

    printf("Read memory of pid %d at address 0x1000, content: %s\n", pid, (char*)readMem);
    return 0;
}
```

You can compile this code and use it to read the first 256 bytes of memory of a process. The address from where to read (0x1000 in example) must be adapted to a valid address of the other process. Note that you must have proper permissions to do so.

Please note that `taskgated` daemon must be configured to allow this and you might need to tweak some system settings or run your program with root permissions. Proceed with caution as this is sensitive territory and may result in crashes if you attempt to read/write memory incorrectly.

