#include <string.h>

bool is_palindrome(char *str) {
    int left = 0;
    int right = strlen(str) - 1;

    while (left < right) {
        // Ignore spaces and move pointers inward
        if (str[left] == ' ') {
            left++;
            continue;
        }
        if (str[right] == ' ') {
            right--;
            continue;
        }

        // Compare characters, case-insensitive
        if (tolower(str[left]) != tolower(str[right])) {
            return false;
        }

        left++;
        right--;
    }

    return true;
}

int main() {
    char str[] = "A man a plan a canal Panama";
    if (is_palindrome(str)) {
        printf("The string is a palindrome.\n");
    }
    else {
        printf("The string is not a palindrome.\n");
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This code segment first initializes two pointers, `left` and `right`, to point to the first and last characters of the input string. Then, it enters a while loop that continues until the two pointers meet in the middle. Within the loop, the code ignores spaces and moves the pointers inward. If the characters at the `left` and `right` pointers don't match (ignoring case differences), the function returns `false`. If the loop completes and all matched pairs of characters were the same, the function returns `true`."}]

